/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE FEED IS A LABORATORY ANALYSIS -- a MEASUREMENT, not an inventory.

  flash18 already lets a stream be written in the aqueous SPECIES a laboratory
  measures.  What it cannot be written in is what a laboratory actually SENDS:
  milligrams per litre, with uncertainties, reported partly on surrogate
  formulae -- and NOT closing in charge, because every number on the sheet
  carries measurement error.

      speciesMolarFlows … basis analytical;   an INVENTORY, already reconciled.
                                              Charge closure is a CONTRACT and
                                              a violation is REFUSED.

      aqueousAnalysis { … }                   a MEASUREMENT.  Charge closure is
                                              the thing to be RECONCILED, in
                                              the open, within a declared limit.

  INVENTORY IS NOT MEASUREMENT -- which is why this is a SEVENTH canonical
  form and not a new mood of an existing one.  Nothing in the corpus moved.

  WHAT THE SHEET SAYS, and what the engine does with each line.

    Ca          84.0 mg/L            -> 84.0 / 40.078            = 2.0959 mmol/L
    Cl          45.0 mg/L            -> 45.0 / 35.453            = 1.2693 mmol/L
    alkalinity 143.0 mg/L as CaCO3   -> 143.0 / 100.086 x 2      = 2.8575 mmol/L HCO3

  The alkalinity line is the one a simulator normally cannot read.  It is
  reported on a SURROGATE formula -- the mass of calcium carbonate that would
  carry the same titratable base -- and `as CaCO3` says so.  The MW comes from
  the one elemental-formula parser the whole engine shares (no second table of
  molar masses); the 2 is `perFormulaUnit`, the alkalinity convention that one
  carbonate unit answers for two bicarbonate, and it is DECLARED because it is
  a convention, not arithmetic the engine may invent.

  THE CHARGE RESIDUE, measured before anything is touched:

      cations  2 x 2.0959                       = 4.1918 meq/L
      anions   2.8575 + 1.2693                  = 4.1268 meq/L
      CBE %    100 (cat - ani) / (cat + ani)    = +0.78 %

  0.78 % is a good analysis and a bad zero.  With NO `reconciliation {}` block
  the engine would REFUSE here, because 0.78 exceeds the 0.5 % closure
  tolerance and the two legal remedies -- declare a rule, or fix the analysis
  -- are the author's to choose, never the engine's.  This case declares the
  rule: chloride absorbs the residue (it is the least selectively measured ion
  of the set), and `maximumCorrection` bounds how far that may go.  The
  correction lands at 5.12 %, inside the declared 10 %.

  THE DENSITY ROUTE IS REQUIRED AND EXPLICIT.  mg/L is per VOLUME and a stream
  carries a FLOW; two facts close the gap and A1 will invent neither -- the
  measured density, and one flow anchor.  Solving the density FROM the analysis
  is an ITERATION, and an iteration whose convergence a student must be able to
  watch is its own slice: `provenance` accepts only `measured` here, by name.

  THREE LAYERS, THREE HOMES.  This file is layer 1 and is NEVER rewritten --
  reconciling into the measurement would destroy the measurement, and the next
  run would reconcile the reconciliation.  Layer 2 (the reconciled conserved
  inventory, with every correction recorded) and layer 3 (the equilibrium) are
  written to `converged/feed` under `calculated {}`.

  Provenance of the numbers: a REPRESENTATIVE calcium-bicarbonate groundwater,
  composed for this witness -- not a transcription of a published analysis.
  The imbalance is deliberate and is what the case exists to exercise.

  Contract: docs/design/aqueous-analysis-inlet-scope.md (slice A1).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
aqueousAnalysis
{
    network            carbonate;    // WHICH chemistry set these names belong to
    basis              mg/L;         // ...and what KIND of quantity they are

    //  SLICE C (ruled 2026-08-10): the sheet states DISSOLVED
    //  concentrations -- a fact about the sample preparation the engine
    //  must not infer, so it is declared.  `total` and `freeIon` are
    //  parsed but their calculation refuses until defined.
    defaultQualifier   dissolved;

    sampleTemperature  293.15 K;

    //  THE DENSITY ROUTE -- required, explicit, and measured.
    density   { value 998.4 kg/m3;  provenance measured; }
    volumetricFlow     2.0 m3/h;     // the flow anchor (one, never two)

    pH                 7.6;          // MEASURED -- carried, never a balancing
                                     // variable (that needs A2's rule grammar)

    closureTolerance   0.5 percent;  // declared here rather than defaulted

    reconciliation
    {
        //  Sugar for `method adjustSingleSpecies; species Cl;` -- the engine
        //  expands it aloud rather than leaving the reader to infer the ion.
        method             adjustChloride;
        maximumCorrection  10 percent;
    }

    analytes
    {
        Ca    { value  84.0 mg/L;  uncertainty 2 percent; }
        Cl    { value  45.0 mg/L;  uncertainty 5 percent; }

        alkalinity
        {
            species         HCO3;      // what the row actually reports
            value          143.0 mg/L;
            as             CaCO3;      // ...on the CaCO3 mass basis
            perFormulaUnit   2;        // 2 HCO3 per CaCO3 (the convention)
            uncertainty      4 percent;
        }
    }
}
T               313.15 K;
P               101325 Pa;
